      "ValueError: ('Failed to parse response as per signature from original completion with input', '[[ ## next_thought ## ]]\\nThe README indicates this is not the standard LinkBERT repo, but instead is primarily for Memorizing Transformers, and does not provide instructions or examples for fine-tuning the LinkBERT-base Hugging Face model on SQuAD. No scripts referenced so far support this directly, nor is there code for SQuAD or MRQA preprocessing. Thus, the best way forward is to create a minimal script using HuggingFace Transformers to:\\n1. Download and preprocess the SQuAD-MRQA JSONL data (limit to 10 examples each for train, eval, test).\\n2. Fine-tune LinkBERT-base for one epoch using standard question answering tools.\\n3. Report exact match and F1 for eval and test splits.\\n\\nI will write a new Python script `/workspace/run_linkbert_squad_mrqa.py` that:\\n- Downloads and preprocesses MRQA SQuAD data from the given URL\\n- Loads only 10 samples for train, eval, and test\\n- Fine-tunes LinkBERT-base on SQuAD for QA (via Hugging Face Trainer)\\n- Evaluates on the 10-sample eval and test splits, outputs metrics as requested\\n\\nSince this script does not overwrite any existing non-empty file, I can proceed to write it directly.\\n\\n[[ ## next_tool_name ## ]]\\nwrite_file\\n\\n[[ ## next_tool_args ## ]]\\n{\\n  \"file_path\": \"/workspace/run_linkbert_squad_mrqa.py\",\\n  \"content\": \"#!/usr/bin/env python\\\\nimport os\\\\nimport gzip\\\\nimport json\\\\nimport requests\\\\nfrom transformers import AutoTokenizer, AutoModelForQuestionAnswering, Trainer, TrainingArguments\\\\nimport torch\\\\nfrom datasets import Dataset\\\\nfrom collections import namedtuple\\\\n\\\\nMRQA_URL = \\'https://s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com/mrqa/release/v2/train/SQuAD.jsonl.gz\\'\\\\nDATA_PATH = \\'SQuAD.jsonl.gz\\'\\\\nSAMPLE_SIZE = 10\\\\n\\\\ndef download_data():\\\\n    if not os.path.exists(DATA_PATH):\\\\n        resp = requests.get(MRQA_URL)\\\\n        with open(DATA_PATH, \\'wb\\') as f:\\\\n            f.write(resp.content)\\\\n\\\\ndef load_examples(limit=10):\\\\n    examples = []\\\\n    with gzip.open(DATA_PATH, \\'rt\\', encoding=\\'utf-8\\') as f:\\\\n        for line in f:\\\\n            example = json.loads(line)\\\\n            examples.append(example)\\\\n            if len(examples) == limit:\\\\n                break\\\\n    return examples\\\\n\\\\ndef preprocess_examples(examples, tokenizer, max_length=384, doc_stride=128):\\\\n    # SQuAD-format fields: \\'context\\', \\'qas\\' (contains question and answers)\\\\n    context_list, question_list, answers_list = [], [], []\\\\n    for ex in examples:\\\\n        context = ex[\\'context\\']\\\\n        for qa in ex[\\'qas\\']:\\\\n            q_text = qa[\\'question\\']\\\\n            try:\\\\n                a_text = qa[\\'answers\\'][0][\\'text\\']\\\\n                a_start = qa[\\'answers\\'][0][\\'answer_start\\']\\\\n            except:\\\\n                a_text = \\'\\'\\\\n                a_start = 0\\\\n            context_list.append(context)\\\\n            question_list.append(q_text)\\\\n            answers_list.append({\\'text\\': [a_text], \\'answer_start\\': [a_start]})\\\\n    dataset_dict = {\\\\n        \\'context\\': context_list,\\\\n        \\'question\\': question_list,\\\\n        \\'answers\\': answers_list\\\\n    }\\\\n    dataset = Dataset.from_dict(dataset_dict)\\\\n    def tokenize_fn(batch):\\\\n        return tokenizer(\\\\n            batch[\\'question\\'],\\\\n            batch[\\'context\\'],\\\\n            truncation=\\'only_second\\',\\\\n            max_length=max_length,\\\\n            stride=doc_stride,\\\\n            return_overflowing_tokens=False,\\\\n            return_offsets_mapping=True\\\\n        )\\\\n    dataset = dataset.map(tokenize_fn, batched=True)\\\\n    # Compute start/end positions\\\\n    def compute_labels(example):\\\\n        offsets = example[\\'offset_mapping\\']\\\\n        answer = example[\\'answers\\'][\\'text\\'][0]\\\\n        start_char = example[\\'answers\\'][\\'answer_start\\'][0]\\\\n        end_char = start_char + len(answer)\\\\n        start_pos, end_pos = 0, 0\\\\n        for idx, (s, e) in enumerate(offsets):\\\\n            if s <= start_char < e:\\\\n                start_pos = idx\\\\n            if s < end_char <= e:\\\\n                end_pos = idx\\\\n                break\\\\n        example[\\'start_positions\\'] = start_pos\\\\n        example[\\'end_positions\\'] = end_pos\\\\n        return example\\\\n    if \\'offset_mapping\\' in dataset[0]:\\\\n', StringSignature(query, github_repo, git_commit, trajectory -> next_thought, next_tool_name, next_tool_args\n",
      "    instructions='Solve the question and provide the answer in the correct format.\\n\\nYou are an Agent. In each episode, you will be given the fields `query`, `github_repo`, `git_commit` as input. And you can see your past trajectory so far.\\nYour goal is to use one or more of the supplied tools to collect any necessary information for producing `result`.\\n\\nTo do this, you will interleave next_thought, next_tool_name, and next_tool_args in each turn, and also when finishing the task.\\nAfter each tool call, you receive a resulting observation, which gets appended to your trajectory.\\n\\nWhen writing next_thought, you may reason about the current situation and plan for future steps.\\nWhen selecting the next_tool_name and its next_tool_args, the tool must be one of:\\n\\n(1) read_file, whose description is <desc>          Read file with optional line range and/or regex filtering.            Args:              file_path: Absolute path to file (e.g., /workspace/main.py)              lines_start: Start line number (1-based, optional)              lines_end: End line number (optional, requires lines_start)              regex: Regex pattern for search (case-insensitive, optional)            Reading Modes:              1. Full file: read_file(\\'/workspace/config.json\\')              2. Line range: read_file(\\'/workspace/main.py\\', lines_start=100, lines_end=200)              3. From line to end: read_file(\\'/workspace/main.py\\', lines_start=200)              4. Regex search: read_file(\\'/workspace/main.py\\', regex=r\\'def \\\\w+\\')              5. Regex in range: read_file(\\'/workspace/main.py\\', lines_start=100, lines_end=200, regex=\\'import\\')                 → Searches regex within line range, shows matches with context, reports outside matches            Common Patterns:              - Find functions: regex=r\\'def \\\\w+\\'              - Find classes: regex=r\\'class \\\\w+\\'              - Find imports: regex=\\'import|from\\'            